From 7a1b4c3f7a02edb3c1b062dc4cd4675d5840743a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 07:19:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Audit: eight more cracks of the same three kinds

An audit for the bug classes the last week produced, rather than waiting
for each one to cost a session.

UNROUTED COMMANDS - three more, all in the settings popups:
  send_geologist         runs a flag search and takes a serf out of an
                         inventory, same as any other serf request
  set_inventory_resource_mode / set_inventory_serf_mode
                         stop the flag taking resources and clear every
                         serf already heading there
Now NetCmd.send_geologist, inv_res_mode and inv_serf_mode. The inventory
ones resolve player.temp_index locally - that is the local interface's
idea of which building the popup is about - and send the building index,
which both machines agree on.

LOCAL SPEED CHANGES - two:
  ord("p") called game.pause() with no guard, while every other speed
  control had one. game_speed is what Game.update adds to the clock, and
  net_enforce_speed only pins it back at turn boundaries, so up to four
  ticks run at the wrong rate first.
  Exiting to the menu from the options popup called set_speed(0) and
  cf_stand_down() on the live game. That now ends the networked session
  first, which is what leaving means anyway.

LOADING A SAVE mid-session (F9) swapped the whole Game out from under
lockstep while the turn numbers carried on lining up. Disabled while
networked. F5 stays - writing a save changes nothing.

THE CHEAT IS NOT COSMETIC. cf_active is armed locally by typing a name
and never crosses the wire, and it hooks the serf state machine in four
places - siege ticks, fight steps, fight end, and burning an enemy
building instead of capturing it. Two machines disagreeing about it are
running two different fights. It will not arm during a networked game.

DRAW CODE WRITING SIMULATION STATE - the class that produced the
peek_serf_at_pos fix. Two more, both in draw_burning_building and
draw_unharmed_building:
  The burning building drained burning_counter and set Building.u from
  the draw path. burning_counter decides the tick the building is
  deleted on and u is hashed, so a fire you were looking at would burn
  out sooner than the same fire on the peer. Freeserf's own TODO admits
  the duplication. Removing it changes nothing even offline:
  update_buildings sets u = tick every tick for every burning building,
  so the delta here was always zero and both writes were no-ops.
  Building.playing_sfx was set and cleared from the draw path for mills,
  smelters and fires. On a weapon smith that bit is not a sound flag at
  all - the state machine uses it to remember sword-or-shield and
  whether the resources are already taken. The draw code does not touch
  a smith today, so nothing was broken, but it is one added case from
  "what you are looking at changes what the factory makes", and no hash
  would catch it. Sound is the viewport's business and now lives there.
  The mine's elevator read stays a real read: a mine's playing_sfx IS
  set by the simulation, by the miner going down the shaft.

DETECTION - the map, which nothing has ever hashed: ownership and
borders, the paths a road laid, tree growth, mineral amounts, fish, and
which serf each tile holds. Too big to hash whole twelve times a second,
so each turn hashes one sixteenth, chosen by the turn number, and the
whole map is covered every sixteen turns - under two seconds.

And a correction to my own last patch: it added player.flags to the
hash, and bit 3 of that is "a message is waiting". The simulation sets
it on both machines and each interface clears it when its own player
reads the message; nothing in the simulation ever reads it. Hashing it
would have reported a desync the first time anyone dismissed a
notification. Masked out.

Also, per Bob: the status line now ages out after ten seconds. The live
half of that notice - whose turn it is to place a castle, and whether we
are actually held up - is a statement about right now, so it stays for
as long as it is true.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DFktm5o1kLnS2Pgvyp8tD4
